  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1110 PM CDT Wed Sep 16 2026\n\nWater vapor satellite imagery and RAP upper air analysis late \nWednesday evening reveal a strong ridge centered over the Ark-La-\nMiss region and a cut-off low upstream over the southern Pacific \nNorthwest and northern CA. The resulting southwesterly flow across \nthe central Rockies has induced the development of a surface lee \ncyclone in eastern CO that will deepen daytime Thursday leading to \nincreasing south-southwesterly winds across southwest KS. These \nmodestly downsloping winds will combine with strong solar insolation \nto support afternoon temperatures increasing into the mid to \nupper 90s, with a few low 100s are possible within the Red \nHills. Strong subsidence under the upper ridge will also inhibit\nprecipitation chances as HREF members keep all convective \nactivity outside our area. Thursday night, south-southwest winds\nwill remain elevated under partly cloudy skies, limiting \nradiational cooling and therefore Friday morning lows to the \nupper 60s/low 70s.\n\nDaytime Friday, short range ensembles agree the cut-off low over the \nnorthwest CONUS will open, allowing easterly momentum to build as it \nreaches the northern Rockies by 06Z Saturday. While this will \ncoincide with a weakening of the upper level ridge, persistent 15-25 \nmph southwesterly downsloping winds and mostly clear skies will \npromote another summer-like afternoon with highs in the mid/upper \n90s. The slight 500-mb height falls may permit some precipitation \nchances Friday afternoon/evening favoring out northwest zones, \nhowever confidence is not high, and most locations will stay rain \nfree.\n\nSaturday through the end of the period, medium range ensembles \nsuggest the upper level trough will continue to trek along the \nnorthern CONUS as the ridge loses strength. One more day of above \nnormal temperatures is likely on Saturday with highs in the low to \nmid 90s, but a strong cold front sometime late Saturday/early Sunday \nwill bring the first truly cool airmass into the central plains this \nfall season. NBM highs on Sunday plummet into the mid 70s to mid \n80s, and stay locked in that range through at least mid-week. \nPrecipitation chances will also be abundant Sunday through Thursday \nas the NBM paints at least some chance category (25-54%) pops \nsomewhere in our CWA each day, but the clear front-runner will \nbe with the cold fropa when likely (55-74%) and definite \n(75-100%) pops are common.",
